title: What trashing does
tags: Trash
kind: reference
summary: Capture the metarecords, delete them in one revision, then move the bytes — in that order, and why.

Trashing is three steps, and their order is not free.

# ''Capture.'' The client reads the target metarecord, walks ''up'' to record its ancestors, and ''down'' the `mfr_path` forest to record every descendant — each with all its fields — through the daemon's ordinary read endpoints, before anything is touched. Once the metarecords are gone there is nothing left to read.
# ''Delete the metarecords'' — the target and its descendants, never the ancestors (the parent directories stay) — in one revision, through [[POST /repos/:repo/metarecords/trash]].
# ''Move the bytes'' into the trash directory ([[Trash on-disk layout]]): a rename, or a copy then delete when the trash is on another filesystem than the file (an external `.metafolder`), which carries the modification time over. A file, a symlink (the link itself, not its target) or a whole directory can be trashed.

Deleting before moving is what keeps the watcher out of it: when the file
disappears, the watcher looks its path up, finds no metarecord there any more,
and has nothing to orphan. The other order would orphan the metarecords in the
gap between the two steps.

A trashing is ''one revision'', so it is one thing to undo
([[Trash, undo and redo]]). It holds whether or not the path is watched: an
unwatched file loses its metarecord too, instead of keeping one that points at
nothing.

!! What refuses a trashing

* ''A reference from outside.'' When a metarecord ''outside'' the trashed set points at one inside it (a `ref` or `tree_ref` value), deleting it would leave the reference dangling: the daemon refuses and names the referrers, and nothing is written. `mf trash -f --force` overrides it; the GUI does not. References ''inside'' the set do not count: they travel with it and come back together.
* ''The repository root'', always, `--force` or not — and since the metadata goes first, before a single byte has moved.

An inbound `external_ref` or `ref_base` value is not detected: those do not feed
the reverse maps the check reads. The gap is accepted, and named here.

Each deleted metarecord also leaves its duplicate group, as an orphaned one does
(spec-duplicates "Leaving a group").
